# Error Handling Patterns

This project has no server API routes for client-side features like
transcript file upload — parsing and validation happen entirely in the
browser. These patterns apply there and to any server function
(`createServerFn`) that talks to Zapier.

## General Rules

- Wrap operations that can fail (file reads, parsing, external calls) in
  try/catch.
- Never use `catch (e: any)` — use `catch (error: unknown)` and narrow
  the type before using it.
- Never catch an error and silently swallow it — at minimum, surface a
  user-safe message; for genuinely unexpected errors, keep visible state
  (don't let the UI hang or falsely report success).
- Show the operator a sanitized message — never a raw exception, stack
  trace, file path, or dependency version.
- Differentiate expected/operational failures (unsupported file type,
  oversized file, malformed `.vtt`) from unexpected ones — operational
  failures get a specific, clear message; unexpected ones get a generic
  "something went wrong" message, never a stack trace either way.

## Pattern: typed errors for expected failure cases

```typescript
export class UnsupportedFileTypeError extends Error {}
export class FileTooLargeError extends Error {}
export class EmptyTranscriptError extends Error {}
export class MalformedVttError extends Error {}
export class UnreadableFileError extends Error {}
```

Map each typed error to one user-safe message at the UI layer — don't
string-match error text to decide what to show.

## What NOT to Do

- Never expose a parser exception or raw stack trace in the UI.
- Never leave existing state (e.g. the transcript editor's content)
  partially modified when an operation fails or is cancelled — either it
  fully applies, or the prior state is left exactly as it was.
- Never log the content of what failed to parse (e.g. the file's text) —
  logging the error type/name is fine, logging the transcript content is
  not.
